# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Extrapolate to the zero-noise limit with built-in and custom fitting functions {#extrapolate-to-the-zero-noise-limit-with-built-in-and-custom-fitting-functions}"
msgstr "Mag-extrapolate sa zero-noise limit gamit ang built-in at custom na fitting function {#extrapolate-to-the-zero-noise-limit-with-built-in-and-custom-fitting-functions}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now that you have data for each noise scale, you can fit a function to the data and extrapolate back to the zero-noise value to estimate an expectation value of the noise-free observable. There are many ways to fit data, and the right choice of function depends on the nature of the data being sampled. ``GateFolding.postprocess`` provides several built-in extrapolation functions, including ``linear``, ``exponential``, and ``double_exponential``. The `postprocess` method takes a list of extrapolation methods, evaluates them in order, and returns the first value with physically-valid values and finite variance. This means the order in which you specify the extrapolators has a big impact on which extrapolator is chosen. Submitting ``fallback`` as an extrapolator means if no valid answers were found, the expectation value of the smallest noise factor will be chosen and returned. If ``fallback`` is not submitted and no extrapolator yields a physically-valid value, the result with the smallest ``std`` is returned. Under Pauli noise an observable decays exponentially with the noise factor, so with 30 layers (15 Trotter steps, 2 layers per step) of entangling gates the exponential extrapolator is the natural choice; you can also extrapolate linearly, which is a common choice when the decay is small.\n"
msgstr "Ngayong mayroon ka nang data para sa bawat noise scale, maaari kang mag-fit ng function sa data at mag-extrapolate pabalik sa zero-noise na halaga para tantiyahin ang expectation value ng noise-free na observable. Maraming paraan para mag-fit ng data, at ang tamang pagpili ng function ay nakadepende sa katangian ng data na sine-sample. Nagbibigay ang ``GateFolding.postprocess`` ng ilang built-in na extrapolation function, kabilang ang ``linear``, ``exponential``, at ``double_exponential``. Tumatanggap ang method na `postprocess` ng listahan ng mga extrapolation method, sinusuri ang mga ito nang sunud-sunod, at ibinabalik ang unang halaga na may physically-valid na mga halaga at finite na variance. Nangangahulugan ito na malaki ang epekto ng pagkakasunod-sunod ng pagtukoy mo sa mga extrapolator sa kung alin ang mapipili. Ang pagsumite ng ``fallback`` bilang extrapolator ay nangangahulugang kung walang nakitang valid na sagot, pipiliin at ibabalik ang expectation value ng pinakamaliit na noise factor. Kung hindi isinumite ang ``fallback`` at walang extrapolator na nagbigay ng physically-valid na halaga, ibinabalik ang resulta na may pinakamaliit na ``std``. Sa ilalim ng Pauli noise, exponential na bumababa ang isang observable ayon sa noise factor, kaya sa 30 layer (15 Trotter step, 2 layer bawat step) ng mga entangling gate, ang exponential extrapolator ang natural na pagpipilian; maaari ka ring mag-extrapolate nang linear, na karaniwang pagpipilian kapag maliit ang pagbaba.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To use a custom fitting function, pass the `\"custom\"` extrapolator together with a `custom_fit` tuple containing the fitting function, a list with an initial guess for each of its parameters, and bounds. These are forwarded to ``scipy.optimize.curve_fit``. Post-processing can be repeated with different extrapolators without re-executing the program. Here you use a custom square-root fitting function. To visualize the extrapolation, plot the site-averaged magnetization calculated at each noise factor for $\\theta = 0.15\\pi$, the three fits, and the site average of the extrapolated expectation values at the zero-noise point. The three data points look nearly linear, yet the linear extrapolation lands visibly below the exact value. As predicted, the exponential fit most-effectively estimates the noiseless expectation value.\n"
msgstr "Para gumamit ng custom na fitting function, ipasa ang `\"custom\"` na extrapolator kasama ng isang `custom_fit` tuple na naglalaman ng fitting function, isang listahan na may paunang hula para sa bawat parameter nito, at mga bound. Ipinapasa ang mga ito sa ``scipy.optimize.curve_fit``. Maaaring ulitin ang post-processing gamit ang iba't ibang extrapolator nang hindi muling pinapatakbo ang program. Dito, gumagamit ka ng custom na square-root na fitting function. Para makita ang extrapolation, i-plot ang site-averaged na magnetization na kinalkula sa bawat noise factor para sa $\\theta = 0.15\\pi$, ang tatlong fit, at ang site average ng mga na-extrapolate na expectation value sa zero-noise na punto. Halos linear ang hitsura ng tatlong data point, pero kitang-kitang mas mababa sa eksaktong halaga ang linear extrapolation. Gaya ng inaasahan, ang exponential fit ang pinakaepektibong nagtatantiya ng noiseless na expectation value.\n"

